rcq("R04-F-08-3", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following best describes how the author interprets the crowds who broke street lamps?",
    "As probably sensing, even if they could not say so, that the lamps carried an authority they had grounds to oppose.",
    [("As defending the freedom that thieves and other night dwellers had enjoyed before the lamps were put up.",
      "The author does not say the crowds acted for thieves; the reading offered concerns resistance to authority, not any particular group's interests."),
     ("As giving clear proof that most townspeople opposed street lighting from the moment it was installed.",
      "The author offers a hedged reading of some crowds 'in moments of unrest'; paragraph 4 adds that many people gained from lighting."),
     ("As vandals whose actions have been wrongly dignified by historians eager to find political resistance.",
      "The author says it is 'tempting to dismiss this as mere vandalism' and then rejects that reading as less plausible.")],
    "Paragraph 3: the lamp-breaking 'is more plausibly read as a recognition, however inarticulate, that the lamps were an extension of an authority that the crowd had reason to resist'. The correct answer keeps the hedge ('more plausibly', 'however inarticulate').",
    "Upgrading the author's hedged reading into a certainty, or attributing to the author the dismissal the author rejects.",
    "The wrong options differ from the correct answer mainly in degree and in whose interests are named; the hedge must be noticed.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

rcq("R04-F-08-4", "Application / analogy", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following is most closely analogous to what the author says, in paragraph 5, that lighting did to the night?",
    "A beach once used at dawn only by fishermen, under their own customs, becomes a public promenade with council rules, drawing families but ending the fishermen's ways.",
    [("A public library extends its opening hours into the evening so that its regular readers can stay and read for longer after work.",
      "This lengthens an existing activity for the same users under the same rules; it captures only the liberation story, not the replacement of a distinct time."),
     ("A council installs cameras in an empty square that nobody had used at night, so that the area can be monitored by the police.",
      "This captures surveillance only, and the square had no prior users or customs to displace, so there is no distinct time being abolished."),
     ("A town holds a festival one night a year at which ordinary rules are suspended and people may behave as they never would by day.",
      "This creates a distinct time with its own rules, which is the reverse of abolishing one."),],
    "Paragraph 5 says lighting abolished the night 'as a distinct social time': 'the day's rules, the day's people and the day's activities spread into hours that had once belonged to others'. The beach case has a distinct prior use with its own customs, an extension of general rules, new users who gain, and the old users' ways lost.",
    "Choosing an analogy for only one of the two stories the author rejects (liberation or discipline).",
    "Each wrong option models one partial reading; the correct answer requires all elements of the paragraph 5 synthesis.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=HARD)

